## Getting started

```bash
bin/setup            # gems, .env, git hooks, database — idempotent, safe to re-run
bin/check            # THE gate: lint, tests, magik check, and this app's own rules
```

### What works today, and what does not

The **framework** is unimplemented; the **tooling** is not. That distinction is real, and it is the
difference between a scaffold you can start working in and one you can only read.

| Works today | Does not — `planned` |
|---|---|
| `bundle install` · `bin/setup` (gems, `.env`, git hooks) | `magik server` · `magik console` · `bin/dev` |
| `rake test` · `rake rubocop` · `bundle exec rubocop` | `magik test` · `magik check` · `magik db …` |
| `bin/check` — it runs lint, the suite and every rule in `scripts/checks/`, and reports the magik steps as `MISSING` rather than pretending | `magik generate …` — write files by hand until it lands |
| `scripts/checks/*.rb` — plain Ruby over plain files, no framework needed | booting, migrating, rendering, serving |
| `magik docs path` · `magik docs list` — the DSL reference, versioned with your gem | everything else in `magik help` marked `planned` |

`bin/check` exits `69` (`EX_UNAVAILABLE`) rather than `1` when a step is missing rather than broken,
so "not built yet" and "your app is wrong" never look the same to a script. Re-derive all of it:

```bash
magik help          # every command, marked `ready` or `planned`
bin/check --list    # every step in the gate
```

## Your own rules are part of the gate

`scripts/checks/` holds this team's rules as executable checks, discovered by `bin/check` and run on
every change. **When the developer is an agent, a convention that is not executable does not
exist** — a line in `CLAUDE.md` is advice on feature #40, and a check is a gate failure with a
runnable fix. Three working examples ship with the app; add your own.
[`scripts/README.md`](scripts/README.md) has the how.

## Layout

```text
app/          models · screens · components · actions · jobs · channels · ledgers
config/       app.rb is the composition root — App.define :<%= app_class %>
db/           migrations (hand-written, append-only) · schema.rb (generated) · seeds.rb
<% if layout == :domains -%>
domains/      one per bounded context; each nests the same app/ tree
<% end -%>
locales/      one file per locale
test/         mirrors app/ exactly, one file per declaration
docs/         README · ARCHITECTURE · FEATURE · PLAN
bin/          the verbs: setup · check · dev · console · test · magik
scripts/      YOUR rules, as executable checks that bin/check discovers
CLAUDE.md     the contract for an agent working here (AGENTS.md is a symlink to it)
llms.txt      this app's link map
```

**One declaration per file**, and the path tells you what the file declares before you open it:
`app/actions/void_invoice.rb` declares `action :void_invoice` and nothing else.

## Working on it with an agent

`CLAUDE.md` is the contract; `.claude/` carries the harness.

| Command | Does |
|---|---|
| `/setup-project` | turn this generic scaffold into your project. **Run it first**, once |
| **`/feature [what a user can do]`** | **the loop.** One slice end to end, ending with the gate green. No argument takes the next item from `docs/PLAN.md`. This is the command you run over and over |
| `/screen <Name>` | one screen or component |
| `/check` | the gate, with every finding triaged to a file and a fix |
| `/next` | the single next task, read from `docs/PLAN.md` |

Six agents sit behind those, scoped by directory so two can run without colliding:
`data-modeler`, `action-author`, `screen-builder`, `ledger-author`, `test-writer`,
`guardrail-reviewer`. See [`.claude/README.md`](.claude/README.md).

## The rules that are not negotiable

Money is integer cents — no `Float`, anywhere near currency. Every model is scoped by `tenant_id`;
every primary key is UUIDv7. An action is the only thing that mutates, a job the only thing that
runs async, a ledger the only thing that moves money, and a screen never queries the database.
Server-rendered HTML plus htmx — no SPA, no offline, no heavy client-side compute. Minitest, never
RSpec. These are enforced at boot, not by review.

